{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9bdfc07b-8ff1-4e95-9c88-de7440fd6192","agentId":"a74910df2cdb4f460","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are an independent, BLIND critic in an adversarial review (\"gauntlet\") of a skill framework for autonomous AI agents that will build a brand-new game engine from scratch, able to power everything from small 2D indie games to future AAA open-world online games. The framework is NOT the engine design. It is: (1) a capability map of everything needed to build and ship the engine, (2) a hierarchy of skills that own those capabilities, (3) contracts between skills, (4) a critic framework. Later, each skill node will become a SKILL.md for a specialized agent. You judge whether the framework is COMPLETE and CORRECTLY BOUNDED. You did not build it and will not be told why choices were made beyond what the artifact says.\n\nARTIFACT (read it; data/*.json is the source of truth, docs are generated views plus a hand-written rationale):\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/data/capabilities.json  (capability map: [id, name, owner, maturity E/M/X/S, contributors])\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/data/skills.json  (skills, profiles, configurations)\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/data/contracts.json\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/data/critics.json, crosscutting.json, seed-map.json\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/docs/00-design-principles.md (rationale — attack it)\n- /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/docs/01..05 (generated views: capability map, dependency graph, hierarchy+skill cards+configuration matrix, cross-cutting, critic framework)\n- The gauntlet protocol for this framework review: /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/PROTOCOL.md (you may read this one file; do not read anything else under gauntlet/)\nBLIND RULE: do NOT open anything else under engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/ except to write your output file, and do not read other skills in the repository.\n\nTHE BAR (from the mission brief):\n- Must support: indie, 2D, 2.5D, modern 3D, open-world, multiplayer, large-scale simulation, high-end PC, console-class, current and future AAA. Must scale DOWN efficiently as well as up; modularity/configurability first-class; not every configuration needs every subsystem.\n- Must NOT default to architectures of Doom/Quake/early Unreal/early Unity, single-threaded engines, scene-graph-centric designs, immediate-mode resource management, legacy OpenGL-era rendering. Current high-end engines (UE5, Frostbite, Snowdrop, Unity DOTS, id Tech, Decima, console engines) are capability references, not designs to copy.\n- \"Best practice\" = strongest currently demonstrated approach (production experience, hardware trends, publications), not most common historically. Techniques must be separated into established / emerging / experimental / speculative; experimental must not become baseline without justification.\n- Hardware: many-core, SMT, heterogeneous cores, wide SIMD, discrete + integrated GPUs, explicit APIs, GPU-driven execution, async compute, HW ray tracing, mesh shaders, work graphs, bindless, sampler feedback, sparse resources, GPU decompression, NVMe, unified memory, NUMA, consoles, desktops, portables.\n- Philosophy: data-oriented design, explicit ownership, deterministic lifetimes, cache efficiency, minimal synchronization, lock-free where justified, job systems, task graphs, fibers/coroutines, async pipelines, parallel init and asset processing, streaming, ECS where appropriate (not dogmatic), dependency-driven execution, determinism where required, observability, profiling from day one, testability, modularity, replaceable subsystems, explicit contracts.\n- Hierarchy: derived from responsibilities, coupling, ownership, required expertise; a small set of orchestration/architecture skills and many focused experts; avoid one giant skill and avoid hundreds of microscopic skills without independent responsibility. Non-responsibilities must prevent overlapping agents modifying the same territory.\n- Definition of done: complete domain map; every major domain owned; no unexplained overlap; explicit dependencies; cross-cutting architecture represented; production tooling as thorough as runtime; dedicated ownership for testing/validation and performance; modern hardware incorporated; recent rendering and systems research considered; networking, audio, physics, input, controllers, tooling, editor, build, deployment, platform covered; legacy assumptions removed or justified; experimental separated from production-ready; the hierarchy can realistically coordinate many autonomous implementation agents. Also: \"A subsystem that performs well but cannot be debugged or tested is not production ready.\" Every skill must eventually specify Validation (\"how an independent critic can objectively determine whether the implementation is correct\"), Performance Validation and Scalability Validation.\n\nALREADY MACHINE-VERIFIED (do not re-derive; you may run `python3 /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/scripts/check.py`): each capability has exactly one existing owner; every skill owns >=1 capability; contract build-dependencies are acyclic and never point to a higher layer; consumed contracts exist; each named configuration is closed under required dependencies; every skill has a domain critic; every brief term maps to capabilities. The checker only proves structure — it cannot tell whether an owner is the RIGHT owner, whether something is MISSING, or whether a boundary is WRONG. That is your job.\n\nYOUR ROLE: Quality & Security Critic.\nMandate: testing, validation, robustness and security — can an independent critic objectively determine correctness of each subsystem? Check: test ownership (strategy, frameworks, contract tests between skills, integration tests, sample projects as tests, rendering validation, physics/animation/audio/network validation methods — e.g. how is audio correctness or netcode correctness validated, who owns simulation validation against analytic references), determinism and concurrency testing, fuzzing of every untrusted input, fault injection, soak, compatibility matrix, certification pre-checks, test infrastructure (device farms, GPU farms, flake management), test data/content ownership, test oracles (reference renderers, reference simulators, golden data), validation of AI agents' own output (the implementers are autonomous agents — is there ownership for verifying agent-produced code, preventing test-gaming / vacuous tests, mutation testing, review gates?); the critic framework itself (are the 14 critics and stages sufficient, is any skill effectively unreviewed for its specialty, is the G1 convergence rule sound and resistant to gaming?). Security: threat model coverage (network, saves, mods/UGC, scripts, assets, editor plugins, supply chain, build infra, telemetry/privacy, anti-cheat, DRM/anti-tamper, secrets, platform keys, server infra boundary, LLM/generative features prompt injection if present), memory-safety posture, sandboxing, trust boundaries represented as contracts, security testing ownership, incident response/vulnerability handling for shipped games and the engine.\n\nOUTPUT: write your findings to /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-1/K-QUALITY.md, one block per finding:\n### K-QUALITY-<n> · <blocker|major|minor> · <omission|overlap|wrong-boundary|wrong-owner|obsolete-assumption|missing-contract|dependency-error|maturity-error|scale-down|other>\n- Target: <capability / skill / contract ids>\n- Finding: <concrete deficiency>\n- Evidence: <technical reasoning; production precedent; name talks/papers/specs where you can>\n- Proposed change: <concrete data change: new capability id + owner, move X from A to B, new/changed contract, split/merge a skill, change a profile tag…>\nSeverity: blocker = framework cannot coordinate agents correctly; major = material omission or wrong boundary causing rework or silent legacy architecture; minor = small placement/naming issue. Rules: only concrete, material deficiencies within your mandate; no stylistic preferences; no praise; do not pad. At most ~20 findings, most severe first. If you find nothing material, write that explicitly.\nYour final message: one line with counts by severity and the output path."},"uuid":"f62094ec-b4be-4cae-ada6-b84f5842a07f","timestamp":"2026-09-29T15:48:40.278Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
