{"parentUuid":"88023b4b-283b-4a0f-b8b0-efe15042f911","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9bdfc07b-8ff1-4e95-9c88-de7440fd6192","agentId":"a74910df2cdb4f460","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L7EuF7ZFy6Fh6Ys8PS8eYf","type":"tool_result","content":"19:    [X] test-architect  (7)\n20:            render-validation  (4)\n22:            robustness-fuzzing  (4)\n24:    [X] security-engineering  (8)\n153:| test-architect | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n154:| render-validation | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● |  | ● |\n156:| robustness-fuzzing | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n158:| security-engineering | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n293:- **Not responsible for:** Making the architecture decisions themselves → engine-architect; Grading sources and running spikes → research-evidence; Test definition of done → test-architect\n327:Owns performance as a discipline: the hypothesis → instrumentation → measurement → optimization → regression-test loop, budgets per subsystem and hardware tier, the scalability / device-profile framework, and performance gates.\n386:### test-architect\n388:**Test Architect** · cross-cutting · parent: engine-architect · profiles: all\n390:Test strategy and architecture: per-subsystem test pyramid, frameworks, the test definition of done every skill must meet, contract tests, test content, coverage and mutation policy, flakiness policy.\n393:- **Contributes to:** `CNT.COOK.determinism-check`; `UI.A11Y.validation`; `BLD.CI.gating`; `PRF.BENCH.workloads`; `XC.DX.doc-tests`\n394:- **Not responsible for:** Running pipelines → ci-cd-automation; Performance regression → perf-benchmarking; Rendering image validation → render-validation\n395:- **Provides:** C-TEST · **Consumes:** —\n396:- **Expertise:** test strategy, contract testing, mutation testing, test determinism\n399:### render-validation\n401:**Rendering Validation** · expert · parent: test-architect · profiles: client\n403:Rendering correctness validation: golden-image tests with perceptual metrics, tolerances per GPU/driver, comparisons against the reference path tracer, graphics API validation in CI.\n405:- **Owns:** `QA.RENDER.golden`; `QA.RENDER.matrix`; `QA.RENDER.reference`; `QA.RENDER.api-validation`\n406:- **Contributes to:** `RND.MAT.validation`; `QA.CERT.photosensitivity`\n407:- **Not responsible for:** Fixing rendering features → render-architect; Material furnace tests (defines them; this skill runs them) → material-system\n414:**Functional Automation & Soak** · expert · parent: test-architect · profiles: all\n416:Game-level automation: scripted and bot playthroughs, smoke/boot tests, soak and stability runs (memory growth, leaks), hardware/OS compatibility matrix, network harnesses and server load tests.\n420:- **Not responsible for:** Unit test frameworks → test-architect; Device-farm infrastructure → ci-cd-automation\n425:### robustness-fuzzing\n427:**Robustness & Fuzzing** · expert · parent: test-architect · profiles: all\n429:Robustness engineering: fuzzing every untrusted-input parser (assets, packets, saves, scripts, mods), sanitizer build policy, fault injection (IO errors, OOM, device lost), concurrency stress and schedule perturbation.\n431:- **Owns:** `QA.ROBUST.fuzzing`; `QA.ROBUST.sanitizers`; `QA.ROBUST.faults`; `QA.ROBUST.concurrency`\n433:- **Not responsible for:** Threat model → security-engineering; Fixing parsers → serialization-schema\n435:- **Expertise:** libFuzzer/AFL++, sanitizers, fault injection, TSan\n440:**Certification & Compliance** · expert · parent: test-architect · profiles: all\n442:Tracks and pre-validates external requirements: platform certification (TRC/XR/Lotcheck class), age ratings, privacy law, accessibility law (CVAA, EAA), third-party licenses and SBOM, photosensitivity testing.\n451:### security-engineering\n453:**Security Engineering** · cross-cutting · parent: engine-architect · profiles: all\n455:Threat modeling and trust boundaries for every untrusted input, secure-coding and memory-safety policy, supply-chain security, secrets, anti-tamper boundary, privacy engineering, mod/UGC sandbox policy.\n457:- **Owns:** `XC.SEC.threats`; `XC.SEC.coding`; `XC.SEC.trust`; `XC.SEC.supply-chain`; `XC.SEC.secrets`; `XC.SEC.tamper`; `XC.SEC.privacy`; `XC.SEC.sandbox`\n459:- **Not responsible for:** Cheat detection → anti-cheat-integrity; Fuzzing campaigns → robustness-fuzzing; Transport encryption implementation → network-transport\n461:- **Expertise:** threat modeling (STRIDE), memory safety, SLSA/SBOM, privacy engineering\n466:**Anti-Cheat & Game Integrity** · expert · parent: security-engineering · profiles: online\n468:Cheat resistance for online games: server-authoritative validation patterns (movement, hits, economy), rate limiting, anomaly signals, integration boundary for client anti-cheat middleware.\n470:- **Owns:** `XC.SEC.server-validation`; `XC.SEC.anticheat`; `XC.SEC.abuse`\n473:- **Expertise:** cheat taxonomies, server validation, anomaly detection\n519:Owns the reload protocol (change detection, dependency invalidation, state preservation) that assets, shaders, scripts, config and C++ live coding plug into; measures edit → see latency.\n532:Maps XAG/GAG and legal requirements to engine features and owns subtitles and captions, screen-reader/TTS/STT hooks, colorblind and contrast modes, text scaling, motion and photosensitivity options, assist hooks, and their validation.\n534:- **Owns:** `UI.A11Y.requirements`; `UI.A11Y.screen-reader`; `UI.A11Y.subtitles`; `UI.A11Y.vision`; `UI.A11Y.motion`; `UI.A11Y.text-scale`; `UI.A11Y.assists`; `UI.A11Y.validation`\n575:- **Not responsible for:** Sandbox policy → security-engineering; Script VM → scripting-runtime\n584:Documentation and developer experience: API reference, architecture docs, the sample-project ladder (2D → open-world online) that doubles as integration tests, templates, onboarding, doc testing, error-message standards, DX metrics.\n586:- **Owns:** `XC.DX.api-docs`; `XC.DX.arch-docs`; `XC.DX.samples`; `XC.DX.templates`; `XC.DX.onboarding`; `XC.DX.doc-tests`; `XC.DX.errors`\n665:- **Not responsible for:** Scheduling → job-system-task-graph; Concurrency test campaigns → robustness-fuzzing\n739:Binary and text formats, schema evolution and upgraders, in-place loadable layouts, canonical deterministic serialization, portability, and validation of untrusted input.\n855:Asset identity and the content pipeline: asset registry and dependency graph, deterministic incremental per-platform cooking, the cook-step contract, derived-data cache and content-addressed store, distributed cooking, content validation.\n1052:- **Contributes to:** `RND.ARCH.threading`; `RND.SHADER.pso-lists`; `RND.POST.hdr-output`; `QA.RENDER.matrix`; `QA.RENDER.api-validation`; `OBS.CRASH.gpu`\n1101:Material model (OpenPBR-class, layered), material graph → shader code generation, instances and parameters, quality tiers, material validation; evaluates neural materials.\n1103:- **Owns:** `RND.MAT.model`; `RND.MAT.graph`; `RND.MAT.instances`; `RND.MAT.tiers`; `RND.MAT.neural`; `RND.MAT.validation`\n1191:Reference path tracer as ground-truth oracle for validation, real-time path tracing, offline cinematic render mode.\n1195:- **Not responsible for:** Movie render orchestration → cinematics-sequencer; Image comparison tests → render-validation\n1756:Shared node-graph editor for material, animation, VFX, audio, PCG and visual-script graphs: framework, compile/validation contract, debugging, diff and merge.\n1824:- **Not responsible for:** CI orchestration → ci-cd-automation; Supply-chain policy → security-engineering\n\n<!-- GENERATED by scripts/render.py from data/*.json — do not edit by hand -->\n\n# 04 · Cross-Cutting Skills\n\nCross-cutting disciplines own an **obligation** that every other skill must meet, and review every skill against it. They also own shared infrastructure where one exists (the instrumentation API, the test DoD, the budget contract). In phase 2, every generated SKILL.md must contain a section answering each obligation below, or state why it does not apply.\n\n| Concern | Owner | Obligation on every skill |\n|---|---|---|\n| performance | performance-architect | Declare budgets per tier, hot paths, and the benchmark that proves them. |\n| memory usage | memory-allocators | Declare allocation strategy, memory tags, peak/steady budgets and OOM behavior. |\n| cache behavior | cpu-performance | Declare data layouts of hot data and access patterns; justify pointer chasing. |\n| concurrency | concurrency-primitives | Declare threading model, shared state, synchronization and task-graph placement. |\n| latency | frame-orchestration | Declare frame phase placement and latency contribution. |\n| determinism | determinism-replay | Declare the determinism level provided and how it is tested. |\n| observability | observability-telemetry | Declare trace zones, counters and logs; zero cost when compiled out. |\n| debugging | visual-debugging-tools | Declare debug visualizations, inspectors and console commands. |\n| testing | test-architect | Meet the test definition of done (unit, contract, integration, fuzz where untrusted). |\n| scalability | performance-architect | Declare behavior from the smallest to the largest configuration and the scaling knobs. |\n| security | security-engineering | Declare untrusted inputs and validation; follow the trust-boundary contract. |\n| platform portability | platform-architect | No platform code above the PAL; declare capability-tier behavior. |\n| maintainability | architecture-governance | Contracts documented, ADRs recorded, fitness functions pass. |\n| tooling | editor-architect | Declare authoring path, inspectors and editor integration for every user-facing feature. |\n| failure recovery | core-runtime-architect | Follow the error model; declare degradation and recovery behavior. |\n| hot reload | hot-reload-iteration | Declare what reloads live and how state is preserved. |\n| data migration | serialization-schema | Version every persisted format and ship upgraders. |\n| backwards compatibility | api-lifecycle-migration | Classify APIs public/internal and follow deprecation policy. |\n| developer usability | developer-experience-docs | Ship docs, a sample, and actionable error messages. |\n| accessibility | accessibility | Map player-facing features to accessibility requirements. |\n| evidence | research-evidence | Every major decision cites graded evidence and a maturity class. |\n\n## Cross-cutting and orchestration skills\n\n- **engine-architect** (orchestrator) — Owns the engine's overall architecture: requirements and configuration profiles, layering and module boundaries, the contract registry, global conventions, build/integrate/buy decisions and cross-domain arbitration. Final authority on the boundaries between skills.\n- **architecture-governance** (orchestrator) — Runs architecture as an evidence-backed, governed process: ADR lifecycle with revisit conditions, technology radar with maturity tiers and promotion criteria, automated fitness functions (layering, dependency direction, contract conformance), anti-legacy review, coding standard.\n- **program-orchestration** (orchestrator) — Turns architecture into coordinated multi-agent work: decomposes milestones into tasks by skill territory, keeps the ownership ledger and territory locks, sequences integration through vertical slices, routes contract change requests, and schedules critic gates.\n- **research-evidence** (cross-cutting) — Research discipline for every major decision: finds and grades authoritative evidence (production talks, platform docs, peer-reviewed work, reproducible benchmarks), classifies techniques as established / emerging / experimental / speculative, and runs spikes where literature is thin.\n- **performance-architect** (cross-cutting) — Owns performance as a discipline: the hypothesis → instrumentation → measurement → optimization → regression-test loop, budgets per subsystem and hardware tier, the scalability / device-profile framework, and performance gates.\n- **test-architect** (cross-cutting) — Test strategy and architecture: per-subsystem test pyramid, frameworks, the test definition of done every skill must meet, contract tests, test content, coverage and mutation policy, flakiness policy.\n- **security-engineering** (cross-cutting) — Threat modeling and trust boundaries for every untrusted input, secure-coding and memory-safety policy, supply-chain security, secrets, anti-tamper boundary, privacy engineering, mod/UGC sandbox policy.\n- **observability-telemetry** (cross-cutting) — Instrumentation substrate: structured logging, trace zones (CPU, GPU, IO, memory), counters and metrics, profiler integrations, in-game stats, development and live telemetry with privacy consent; zero cost when compiled out.\n- **crash-diagnostics** (cross-cutting) — Crash, hang and GPU-fault capture and reporting: minidumps, symbolication, bucketing and triage, GPU crash dumps, watchdogs, OOM reports, assert reporting.\n- **determinism-replay** (cross-cutting) — Defines determinism levels (none, same-binary, cross-platform) per subsystem and the floating-point and parallel-execution rules that achieve them; owns input/state record-replay and desync detection.\n- **hot-reload-iteration** (cross-cutting) — Owns the reload protocol (change detection, dependency invalidation, state preservation) that assets, shaders, scripts, config and C++ live coding plug into; measures edit → see latency.\n- **accessibility** (cross-cutting) — Maps XAG/GAG and legal requirements to engine features and owns subtitles and captions, screen-reader/TTS/STT hooks, colorblind and contrast modes, text scaling, motion and photosensitivity options, assist hooks, and their validation.\n- **api-lifecycle-migration** (cross-cutting) — Public API surface, semantic versioning and deprecation, ABI policy, engine-upgrade tooling (codemods, data upgraders) and LTS policy, so projects can move across engine versions.\n- **developer-experience-docs** (cross-cutting) — Documentation and developer experience: API reference, architecture docs, the sample-project ladder (2D → open-world online) that doubles as integration tests, templates, onboarding, doc testing, error-message standards, DX metrics.\n\n## Universal contracts\n\n- `C-ERR` Error & failure model — consumed by every runtime skill; owner core-runtime-architect.\n- `C-MEM` Allocation & memory accounting — consumed by every runtime skill; owner memory-allocators.\n- `C-INSTR` Instrumentation — consumed by every runtime skill; owner observability-telemetry.\n- `C-CRASH` Crash & diagnostic hooks — consumed by every runtime skill; owner crash-diagnostics.\n- `C-BUDGET` Budgets & scalability profiles — consumed by every runtime skill; owner performance-architect.\n- `C-ARCH` Architecture, layering & profiles — consumed by every skill; owner engine-architect.\n- `C-ADR` ADR & fitness functions — consumed by every skill; owner architecture-governance.\n- `C-EVID` Evidence grading — consumed by every skill; owner research-evidence.\n- `C-ORCH` Agent coordination protocol — consumed by every skill; owner program-orchestration.\n- `C-TEST` Test & validation definition of done — consumed by every skill; owner test-architect.\n- `C-API` API stability & deprecation — consumed by every skill; owner api-lifecycle-migration.\n- `C-TRUST` Trust boundaries — consumed by every skill; owner security-engineering.\n\n{\"_doc\": \"Every domain term named in the mission brief, mapped to the capabilities that cover it. The checker fails if any term maps to nothing or to an unknown capability. Adding a term here is how a critic's 'missing X' becomes a machine-checked obligation.\", \"brief_domains\": {\"engine architecture\": [\"ARCH.STRUCT.layering\", \"ARCH.STRUCT.contracts\"], \"platform abstraction\": [\"PLAT.PAL.os\"], \"hardware abstraction\": [\"PLAT.PAL.cpu-topology\", \"RND.RHI.backends\", \"RND.RHI.caps\"], \"core runtime\": [\"CORE.LIFE.boot\", \"CORE.LIFE.modules\"], \"memory management\": [\"CORE.MEM.virtual\", \"CORE.MEM.oom\"], \"allocators\": [\"CORE.MEM.allocators\"], \"containers\": [\"CORE.TYPES.containers\"], \"concurrency\": [\"CORE.CONC.sync\", \"CORE.JOBS.scheduler\"], \"synchronization\": [\"CORE.CONC.sync\", \"RND.RHI.sync\"], \"atomics\": [\"CORE.CONC.atomics\"], \"lock-free structures\": [\"CORE.CONC.lockfree\", \"CORE.CONC.reclamation\"], \"job systems\": [\"CORE.JOBS.scheduler\"], \"task graphs\": [\"CORE.JOBS.graph\"], \"fibers/coroutines\": [\"CORE.JOBS.fibers\"], \"ECS\": [\"CORE.ECS.storage\", \"CORE.ECS.queries\"], \"object/entity identity\": [\"CORE.OBJ.identity\"], \"reflection\": [\"CORE.REFL.registry\"], \"metadata\": [\"CORE.REFL.attributes\"], \"serialization\": [\"CORE.SER.binary\", \"CORE.SER.evolution\"], \"configuration\": [\"CORE.LIFE.config\"], \"logging\": [\"OBS.LOG.logging\"], \"diagnostics\": [\"OBS.LOG.remote\", \"OBS.CRASH.hangs\"], \"crash handling\": [\"OBS.CRASH.capture\", \"OBS.CRASH.pipeline\"], \"telemetry\": [\"OBS.LOG.telemetry\"], \"profiling\": [\"OBS.LOG.tracing\", \"OBS.LOG.profilers\"], \"benchmarking\": [\"PRF.BENCH.micro\", \"PRF.BENCH.workloads\"], \"deterministic execution\": [\"XC.DET.levels\", \"XC.DET.parallel\"], \"replay systems\": [\"XC.DET.replay\", \"NET.REP.replays\"], \"time management\": [\"CORE.FRAME.time\"], \"frame scheduling\": [\"CORE.FRAME.pipelining\", \"CORE.FRAME.phases\"], \"resource management\": [\"RES.MGMT.handles\"], \"asset identity\": [\"CNT.ID.identity\"], \"asset pipelines\": [\"CNT.COOK.architecture\"], \"importers\": [\"CNT.IMP.gltf\", \"CNT.IMP.fbx\", \"CNT.IMP.usd\"], \"cooking\": [\"CNT.COOK.architecture\", \"CNT.COOK.contract\"], \"build pipelines\": [\"BLD.CI.orchestration\", \"BLD.SYS.graph\"], \"compression\": [\"RES.PKG.compression\", \"CNT.COOK.textures\", \"ANM.RT.compression\"], \"package formats\": [\"RES.PKG.format\"], \"virtual file systems\": [\"RES.PKG.vfs\"], \"IO\": [\"RES.IO.backends\"], \"asynchronous IO\": [\"RES.IO.backends\", \"RES.IO.scheduling\"], \"streaming\": [\"RES.MGMT.streaming\"], \"world partitioning\": [\"WLD.PART.grid\"], \"level streaming\": [\"WLD.PART.sources\"], \"large-world coordinates\": [\"WLD.PART.lwc-policy\", \"WLD.SPACE.lwc\"], \"scene representation\": [\"WLD.MODEL.strategy\", \"RND.ARCH.scene-sync\"], \"spatial structures\": [\"WLD.SPACE.index\", \"PHY.COL.broadphase\"], \"transforms\": [\"WLD.SPACE.transforms\"], \"visibility\": [\"RND.GEO.culling\"], \"rendering architecture\": [\"RND.ARCH.pipeline\"], \"render graphs\": [\"RND.GRAPH.declare\"], \"GPU scheduling\": [\"RND.GRAPH.async\"], \"graphics resource management\": [\"RND.MEM.heaps\", \"RND.MEM.lifetime\"], \"shader systems\": [\"RND.SHADER.language\", \"RND.SHADER.permutations\"], \"shader compilation\": [\"RND.SHADER.toolchain\"], \"shader reflection\": [\"RND.SHADER.reflection\"], \"materials\": [\"RND.MAT.model\"], \"physically based rendering\": [\"RND.MAT.model\", \"RND.LIGHT.units\", \"RND.POST.camera\"], \"lighting\": [\"RND.LIGHT.clustered\", \"RND.LIGHT.stochastic\"], \"global illumination\": [\"RND.GI.hybrid\", \"RND.GI.probes\", \"RND.GI.baked\"], \"shadows\": [\"RND.LIGHT.vsm\", \"RND.LIGHT.csm\", \"RND.LIGHT.rt-shadows\"], \"virtualized geometry\": [\"RND.LOD.clusters\"], \"level of detail\": [\"RND.LOD.discrete\", \"RND.LOD.hlod\"], \"occlusion\": [\"RND.GEO.culling\"], \"culling\": [\"RND.GEO.culling\"], \"GPU-driven rendering\": [\"RND.GEO.gpu-scene\", \"RND.GEO.indirect\"], \"indirect rendering\": [\"RND.GEO.indirect\"], \"mesh shaders\": [\"RND.GEO.mesh-shaders\"], \"compute pipelines\": [\"RND.GRAPH.compute\"], \"ray tracing\": [\"RND.RT.as\", \"RND.RT.pipelines\"], \"path tracing\": [\"RND.PT.reference\", \"RND.PT.realtime\"], \"post-processing\": [\"RND.POST.effects\"], \"HDR\": [\"RND.P","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"125ee6e8-09a4-4dbf-8cf5-0f1ceb317d1b","timestamp":"2026-09-29T15:49:08